// Contextual-keyword corpus fixture (docs/pmt/language.md): functions
// literally named after pmc's contextual keywords — `export`, `use`,
// `namespace` — none of which sits in the RESERVED definition-name
// vocabulary (parser.rs's `is_reserved_definition_name` only blocks the
// eight RESERVED command words plus `volatile`), so all three parse as
// ordinary function names as long as the keyword's own triggering
// look-ahead doesn't match: `export`/`use` need a following IDENT to
// read as the modifier/import-list respectively, and `namespace` needs
// IDENT then `{`; here each name is immediately followed by `(`
// instead, so every site falls through to an ordinary function
// definition. `export namespace()` pins the sharper two-ident header
// shape too: `export` decodes as the MODIFIER (consumed because it is
// followed by another IDENT), and the ident that follows — itself the
// contextual keyword `namespace` — decodes as the plain function NAME,
// not as a `namespace NAME { … }` block (that pattern only triggers
// when `namespace` itself leads and is followed by IDENT then `{`).
// Plus an aliased import (`end`) that shadows nothing in this file, and
// calls that exercise all three names — through `@name()`, which only
// rejects the eight RESERVED words, not these three contextual ones —
// from `main`.
use std::goToEnd as end;

export() {
    right;
}

use() {
    left;
}

export namespace() {
    mark;
}

main() {
    @export();
    @use();
    @namespace();
    @end();
}
